# Migrating `BackgroundProcessor` to .NET 8

## Critical Breaking Changes

1. **`Thread.Abort()` throws `PlatformNotSupportedException`** — removed entirely in .NET Core+
2. **`ThreadAbortException` will never be thrown** — the `catch` block is dead code
3. **`Thread.ResetAbort()` also throws `PlatformNotSupportedException`**

## Key Pitfalls

| Pitfall | Detail |
|---|---|
| `Thread.Abort()` silent failure | Compiles fine, explodes at runtime |
| Blocking `GetNextItem()` | Without cancellation support, `Stop()` hangs indefinitely |
| `volatile bool` spin-check | Works but `CancellationToken` is idiomatic and cooperates with async APIs |
| `Thread.Sleep(2000)` for shutdown | Brittle; use `Join(timeout)` or async wait |

## Migrated Code (.NET 8)

```csharp
public class BackgroundProcessor : IDisposable
{
    private Task? _workerTask;
    private CancellationTokenSource? _cts;

    public void Start()
    {
        _cts = new CancellationTokenSource();
        _workerTask = Task.Run(() => ProcessLoop(_cts.Token));
    }

    private void ProcessLoop(CancellationToken token)
    {
        while (!token.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            try
            {
                // Pass token so blocking calls become cancellable
                var item = GetNextItem(token);
                ProcessItem(item);
            }
            catch (OperationCanceledException) when (token.IsCancellationRequested)
            {
                // Graceful shutdown requested — replaces ThreadAbortException
                RollbackCurrentTransaction();
                break; // exit loop cleanly; no ResetAbort needed
            }
            finally
            {
                FlushBuffers();
            }
        }
    }

    public void Stop(TimeSpan? timeout = null)
    {
        if (_cts is null || _workerTask is null)
            return;

        _cts.Cancel(); // signal cancellation

        // Wait with a real timeout instead of Thread.Sleep
        if (!_workerTask.Wait(timeout ?? TimeSpan.FromSeconds(5)))
        {
            // Log warning: worker didn't stop in time.
            // In .NET 8 there is NO forced termination equivalent —
            // you must ensure GetNextItem/ProcessItem honor the token.
        }
    }

    public void Dispose()
    {
        Stop();
        _cts?.Dispose();
        _workerTask?.Dispose();
    }
}
```

## What Changed and Why

| .NET Framework | .NET 8 | Reason |
|---|---|---|
| `Thread` + manual bool | `Task.Run` + `CancellationToken` | Structured cancellation; thread pool efficiency |
| `Thread.Abort()` | `CancellationTokenSource.Cancel()` | `Abort()` throws `PlatformNotSupportedException` |
| `catch (ThreadAbortException)` | `catch (OperationCanceledException)` | The replacement cancellation exception |
| `Thread.ResetAbort()` | *Removed* — just `break` | No auto-rethrow behavior to suppress |
| `Thread.Sleep(2000)` | `_workerTask.Wait(timeout)` | Deterministic; doesn't guess |
| Nothing | `IDisposable` | Cleans up `CancellationTokenSource` |

## The Non-Obvious Requirement

The migrated code **only works if `GetNextItem` accepts and honors the `CancellationToken`**. If it blocks on something uncancellable (e.g., a legacy synchronous API), the stop signal can't interrupt it. In that case, wrap the blocking call:

```csharp
private WorkItem GetNextItem(CancellationToken token)
{
    // Bridge a legacy blocking call into something cancellable
    return Task.Run(() => LegacyBlockingGet(), token)
               .GetAwaiter().GetResult();
}
```

> **Warning:** This burns a thread pool thread to unblock cancellation — it's a migration shim, not a long-term solution. Replace the underlying blocking API with an async equivalent when possible.
